WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.820
Greatest challenges that our
social entrepreneurs face

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.430
are clearly identifying
a theory of change, or

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:11.920
a strategy for how they're going to
accomplish what they need to accomplish.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.870
Another is business model innovation,
what is the best business model for

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:18.690
their enterprise.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.470
The Executive Program in
Social Entrepreneurship is a submersive

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:26.020
experience that brings together people
from all over the world with the goal of

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.200
making their organizations more impactful,
and

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.230
better able to serve the needs of
their clients and their customers.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:37.870
&gt;&gt; As a social entrepreneur, you get so
caught up in the mission that you forget

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:44.310
you actually need to continuously
grow as a professional, gain skills.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.490
So that you can keep up
with the development and

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.280
the execution of your mission.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.530
&gt;&gt; I had zero expectations of the kind
of people that I would meet here.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:55.620
And I was really surprised to see
such experienced people coming here,

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.670
and change introductory of their careers.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:01.000
Or just enhance the organization and
then come here with a particular

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.640
problem that they really wanted to
solve in over the course of this week.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.680
&gt;&gt; You've got such a great
diversity of people.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:15.080
And so a lot of what we've been discussing
is around, how do we address those special

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.760
challenges that each of us is facing and
feed off of each other that way.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.130
&gt;&gt; The power of the network
that we build in this program,

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.320
the power of the reciprocity, is enormous.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.560
&gt;&gt; I thought about the problem that
I was facing in regards to a pivot.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.070
When I looked through the curriculum,
I could see that

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.600
the tools are really practical and
that I could take them away and

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.660
immediately apply them to
the work that I was doing.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.860
&gt;&gt; It's a combination of
an institution that's doing

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:50.720
cutting edge research around
social innovation, and

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.950
within a business school very practically
minded how do you really make this lift.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:56.960
&gt;&gt; From the programmer,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.590
directors to the faculty it was really
pleasant to see how approachable they are.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.980
&gt;&gt; The participants leave this program
with a very clear set of guidelines for

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.380
thinking about what it is that they're
trying to do in the world, why they're

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.010
doing it, and why they think they're
going to be successful at doing it.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:17.040
They take away a sense of what
it means to balance social and

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.150
economic goals in a social enterprise.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.020
&gt;&gt; We really need to listen to the people
that we serve, to understand their

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:29.070
challenges, to help design systems and
processes and services for them.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:33.250
&gt;&gt; It's a very intense and inspiring
program, but you have to be full on.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.042
&gt;&gt; I can already see how it's going
to translate in action on the field.

